---
/**
 * foodcraft / TestimonialsBL — "Kalinaw Coffee" IN THEIR WORDS: a CENTRED head
 * (rust eyebrow over a Fraunces headline) above a three-up row of white quote
 * cards on the cream ground, each opened by a rating mark, then the quote set in
 * Fraunces at 19.5px and a tracked-caps attribution.
 *
 * ROLE: the social proof a stranger decides on before walking in — which is
 * exactly why this file prints nothing the owner did not put on record.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * TESTIMONIALS section of "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *   head            text-align centre, 46px below it
 *   eyebrow         10px / .42em / caps / #B4541F / 600      → .kick, unchanged
 *   headline        Fraunces 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.08, margin .26em 0 0
 *   grid            repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 24px
 *   card            #FFFFFF ground, 1px #E9DFCF hairline, radius 16px,
 *                   padding 30px 28px
 *   mark            13px, #B4541F, letter-spacing 3px, 17px below it
 *   quote           Fraunces 19.5px / 1.55, #241A12, 18px below it
 *   attribution     11px / .2em / caps / #948373
 * Mapped to the wrapper tokens that carry those same values: #FFFFFF = --card,
 * #E9DFCF = --line, 16px = --r-lg, #B4541F = --accent, #241A12 = --ink,
 * #948373 = --muted, Fraunces = --disp. NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS BELOW. Nothing is
 * re-derived to "survive" a scheme: this is the cream card the designer drew.
 *
 * NOT A BAND. The design leaves this section on the cream ground — the espresso
 * bands in this template are Trust and How. So this file carries no `band-dark`,
 * and the amber/bone tokens (which exist only for those bands) are untouched
 * here. Adding the band class would be a second design, not this one.
 *
 * READS content.testimonials.items ({quote, who, rating}); tolerates the legacy
 * bare array parked at content.testimonials and the legacy author/name spellings
 * for the attribution. The data-field always names the CANONICAL path, so the
 * first sidebar edit migrates a legacy row onto the real key.
 *
 * COERCED AT EVERY READ — THE ROW FIRST, THEN EVERY FIELD. Nothing upstream type
 * checks either one. astro-builder's normalizeBlock passes a non-object entry
 * straight through (`if (!it || typeof it !== 'object') return it`), so
 * `items` can hold a null, and reading `.quote` off that null throws at BUILD
 * time and takes the whole page down with it. So each row is normalised to an
 * object before a single field is read; a bare STRING row is read as the quote
 * itself — the legacy shape where the row IS the quote — exactly the way AreaBL
 * reads a bare `place`, so owner text is never silently dropped instead.
 * Then the fields: a quote that arrives as a number crashes the build on
 * .trim(), and one that arrives as an object prints "[object Object]" through
 * set:html. str() passes strings and finite numbers and treats everything else
 * as absent. Blank-after-trim counts as absent too — a whitespace-only `who`
 * must not draw an empty figcaption, and a whitespace-only tag must not draw an
 * empty rust eyebrow.
 * astro-builder's normalizeBlock aliases name/author onto `who` only when `who`
 * is null, so a row carrying who:'' keeps its blank; the fallback chain below is
 * `||` and not `??` so that row still prints the name the owner did type.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one is declared in genericContentSchema, group
 * "testimonials" — a data-field on a path the schema does not own is a dead hook
 * that no sidebar input can focus)
 *   testimonials.tag                  — the rust eyebrow
 *   testimonials.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place this
 *                                       template's signature ITALIC RUST
 *                                       EMPHASIS anywhere inside their own
 *                                       sentence (the wrapper styles .h2 em)
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.quote     — set:html, the Fraunces blockquote
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.who       — the tracked-caps figcaption
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.rating    — read, never printed as text: it is the
 *                                       ONLY thing that can draw a star (below).
 *                                       It deliberately carries NO data-field on
 *                                       the star row, and that is not a missing
 *                                       hook — the row RENDERS the number, it is
 *                                       not the number. editorBridge's ed:update
 *                                       would replace the glyphs with the raw
 *                                       digit, and VisualEditor (which does not
 *                                       honour data-derived) makes every
 *                                       [data-field] contentEditable, so an
 *                                       admin could type "★★★★★" back into
 *                                       rating and lose the score. It is edited
 *                                       in the sidebar's "Rating out of 5" box,
 *                                       which genericContentSchema declares.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored: the number of stars, and the reveal delay (capped at 3
 * so a fourth card is not still hidden a second after it scrolls in).
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION — the hard rule of this section:
 *   - THE STAR ROW IS NOT HARDCODED. The design prints "★★★★★" over every quote,
 *     because the designer was filling their own template. Five stars for a
 *     customer who never gave a score is a claim invented on the owner's behalf,
 *     so stars are drawn ONLY from a finite numeric
 *     testimonials.items.${i}.rating. Non-numeric, blank and absent ratings draw
 *     no stars at all; the card instead opens with a rust hairline that occupies
 *     the same beat and states nothing. Expect the hairline on essentially every
 *     real site — that is the correct, honest default, not a missing feature.
 *   - CLAMPING, stated so it is not read as an oversight: a rating is rounded,
 *     then capped at 5 (a typo of "7" draws five stars, never seven). A value
 *     that rounds BELOW 1 — 0, -2, 0.4 — is treated as no usable rating and
 *     draws the hairline. It is never clamped UP to one star, because inventing
 *     a star out of a zero is the same fabrication as inventing five.
 *   - The accessible name announces the SAME number that is drawn, so the label
 *     and the glyphs can never disagree.
 *   - NO review count, NO average score, NO platform name, NO "regulars' choice"
 *     badge. None of it is in the data contract, and the design draws none.
 *   - Quote and attribution are guarded independently, and every guard sits
 *     OUTSIDE its tag, so a quote with no name renders as an unattributed quote
 *     rather than an empty caption holding a bottom margin open.
 *   - A row with no quote is DROPPED: the card IS the quote, so a name-only row
 *     would render a bordered white box attributing nothing to somebody. The row
 *     stays in the sidebar and reappears the instant a quote is typed.
 *
 * SECTION GATE: the whole <section> renders only when at least one row has a
 * real quote. It deliberately does NOT count tag/headline. Those two arrive from
 * the admin sidebar or the AI extractor with nothing behind them all the time,
 * and lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills heads across the site, so a gate that
 * counted a headline would ship a centred heading over an empty grid — the one
 * thing a proof section must never do.
 *
 * ORIGINAL INDEXES: `i` is captured BEFORE blank rows are dropped, so deleting
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 4. Nothing here is numbered
 * on screen, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * THE ONE AUTHORED STRING in this file is the stars' aria-label, which is never
 * visible. It exists because a row of glyphs with no accessible name is
 * decoration announcing nothing, and a rating an owner typed is information a
 * screen-reader user is entitled to. No visible word on this page comes from
 * this file.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   testimonials.source   — the schema declares it, but this template has an
 *                           honest home for a star claim (rating), and the
 *                           design draws no source line under the head. A
 *                           "★ Reviews on Google" line would also be a platform
 *                           claim nothing in the pipeline verifies.
 *   testimonials.bigQuote — there is no pull-quote element in this design.
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.role — the row schema stops at quote/who/rating, so
 *                           the hook would focus nothing in the v3 sidebar.
 *                           (normalizeBlock aliases `context` onto `role`, but
 *                           no sidebar input owns it here.)
 *   No image: the design's card is type only, with no avatar plate — so there is
 *   no .plate, no role="img", and nothing to give an accessible name to.
 *
 * NO ANCHOR HELPER, and that is not an omission: this section emits no <a> at
 * all (the design's testimonials block contains no link), so there is no href to
 * remap onto the page's anchor set and no data-href-field to own. Copying
 * onPage()/PAGE_ANCHORS in here would be an unused function, not a safeguard. It
 * also takes NO id — the page's ids are menu / about / gallery / location / faq
 * / main, and the design gives this section none of them.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME NOTE (engine): this section must NOT be class "testi" —
 * buildOverrideCss pins `.testi, .testi p { color: <on-ink> !important }`, which
 * would repaint the quotes with a scheme colour the design never chose. It is
 * class "regulars" and carries no buttons at all, so no engine label pin reaches
 * it.
 *
 * CONTRAST: the attribution is --muted on --card, ~3.7:1 — below AA. That is the
 * design's own pairing (the wrapper's frontmatter records --muted as accepted
 * rather than silently darkened) and on the pure-white card it is marginally
 * tighter than the wrapper's on-paper figure. The line carries only a name that
 * the quote above it does not depend on, and lifting --muted is a one-line
 * change in the wrapper — the owner's call, not this file's. The stars are
 * --accent on --card, ~5:1, comfortably AA. The quote is --ink on --card, ~15:1.
 *
 * VERTICAL RHYTHM comes from the wrapper's shared `section` padding — the
 * design's "96px top, 0 bottom" is the same rhythm once every section stacks.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const test = content.testimonials || {};

/* Coerce at EVERY read — see COERCED AT EVERY READ above. A string passes; a
   finite number passes as its digits; null, undefined, NaN, an array and an
   object are not content and become ''. Nothing downstream calls a string method
   on raw input, so no shape from the pipeline can fail the build. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const tag = str(test.tag).trim();
const headline = str(test.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  quote?: unknown;
  who?: unknown;
  author?: unknown;
  name?: unknown;
  rating?: unknown;
};

/* Tolerate both shapes at every read: the canonical object with .items, and the
   legacy bare array parked at content.testimonials. */
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(test.items)
  ? test.items
  : (Array.isArray(test) ? (test as Item[]) : []);

/* A rating is a CLAIM about a customer. Only a finite number becomes stars; it
   is rounded, capped at 5, and anything that rounds below 1 yields 0 — which
   draws the hairline, not a star. Never widen this. */
const starsOf = (it: Item): number => {
  const r = Number(it.rating);
  if (!Number.isFinite(r)) return 0;
  const n = Math.round(r);
  return n >= 1 ? Math.min(5, n) : 0;
};

const STAR = '★';

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index, then drop the rows that
   cannot render an honest card. Gating on `rows` rather than `items` also means
   a block of blank rows hides instead of shipping three empty white boxes. */
const rows = items
  .map((raw, i: number) => {
    /* THE ROW IS COERCED BEFORE ANY FIELD IS READ — see COERCED AT EVERY READ
       above. A null row would throw on `.quote` and fail the build; a bare
       string row is the quote the owner typed and is kept, not dropped. */
    const it: Item = raw && typeof raw === 'object' ? (raw as Item) : ({ quote: raw } as Item);
    return {
      /* the quote keeps its own leading/trailing space — only the GUARD is
         trimmed, so a blank-looking quote cannot open a bordered card */
      quote: str(it.quote),
      who: str(it.who).trim() || str(it.author).trim() || str(it.name).trim(),
      stars: starsOf(it),
      i,
    };
  })
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.quote.trim()));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="regulars">
    <div class="wrap">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="rg-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="testimonials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2" data-field="testimonials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class="rg-grid">
        {rows.map((row, k) => (
          <figure class="rg-card reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {row.stars > 0 ? (
              /* Drawn from the owner's own number, and named with that same
                 number so the glyphs are never decoration-only. */
              <span class="rg-stars" role="img" aria-label={`Rated ${row.stars} out of 5`}>
                <span aria-hidden="true">{STAR.repeat(row.stars)}</span>
              </span>
            ) : (
              /* Where the design hardcodes five stars. A rust hairline on the
                 same beat: same visual opening, no claim. */
              <span class="rg-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            )}
            <blockquote data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.quote`} set:html={row.quote}></blockquote>
            {row.who && (
              <figcaption data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.who`}>{row.who}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── head · centred, 46px of air under it (design) ───────────────────────── */
  .rg-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 46px; }
  .rg-head .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The wrapper's .h2 is left-aligned with a bottom margin; the design's head is
     centred with the margin on TOP. The 24ch cap from the wrapper is kept (and
     centred by the auto margins) so a long owner headline cannot run the full
     1240px of the container. */
  .regulars .h2 {
    margin: 0.26em auto 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── grid · three up, 24px (design) ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .rg-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 24px;
  }

  /* ── card · the design's white panel inside a warm hairline, 16px corner ─── */
  .rg-card {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: 30px 28px;
  }

  /* ── the rating mark. Both variants occupy ~39px so a card WITH a rating and a
        card without stay on the same baseline in the same row. ────────────── */
  .rg-stars {
    display: block;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 17px;
  }
  /* the hairline that replaces the design's hardcoded star row — 76px is roughly
     the width those five glyphs occupy at 13px on 3px tracking */
  .rg-rule {
    display: block;
    width: 76px;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--accent);
    margin: 11px 0 27px;
  }

  /* ── quote · Fraunces at the design's 19.5px. The UA gives <blockquote> a 40px
        side margin, so the reset here is load-bearing, not tidying. ────────── */
  .rg-card blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 19.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's <em> is only
     reachable through :global(). It stays the quote's own ink: the italic RUST
     emphasis is the HEADLINE signature, and repainting a customer's words rust
     would read as the café talking, not the customer. */
  .rg-card blockquote :global(em),
  .rg-card blockquote :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: inherit; }

  /* ── attribution · the design's 11px / .2em, not .mono's 10px / .2em / 500 ── */
  .rg-card figcaption {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE · this section owns its own grid; the wrapper owns the shared
        760 / 520 type pass. Nothing here may push the page sideways. ──────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .rg-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 20px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .rg-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .rg-head { margin-bottom: 34px; }
    .regulars .h2 { max-width: none; }
    .rg-card { padding: 24px 20px; }
    .rg-card blockquote { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.5; margin-bottom: 16px; }
    .rg-card figcaption { letter-spacing: 0.16em; }
    .rg-stars { letter-spacing: 2px; }
  }
</style>
